{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Keep application data under /srv/data on storage supporting filesystem snapshots and capture an earlier snapshot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"/srv/data was mounted from an LVM-backed XFS logical volume, representative application data and its content/metadata baseline were recorded, and an LVM snapshot named snap_before_change was successfully created.","evidence_ids":["cmd-129dfc579a624986","cmd-21d1046394b545f9","cmd-06ab08ca720f4246"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Modify /srv/data after capturing the snapshot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The post-snapshot state directly showed changed file hashes and permissions, deleted snapshot-era entries, and a newly added file.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0a75618512064233"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Restore the exact content captured by the earlier snapshot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The snapshot merge completed at 100%. After reactivation and remounting, all recorded hashes passed, the complete tree and metadata matched the baseline, the post-snapshot file was absent, and the original configuration content was restored.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5674dfde392649c0","cmd-db2b7b4d3e184645","cmd-41c83e6c6840413e"]}],"confidence":0.84,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.62,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified in the command timeline: storage creation and persistence configuration supported snapshot recovery; test-data mutations exercised the required recovery; reactivation corrected the post-merge mount condition; and the reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. However, the required reboot left node1 unreachable through the management channel for the remainder of collection, so final service, package, temporary-file, mount, and data hygiene could not be observed. The reboot was acknowledged and followed by connection loss, but recovery was never demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d085541f5a5a4945","cmd-a3b61e5838b34069","cmd-96c8b86d70274ae6","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes were directly demonstrated before the mandatory restart: snapshot-capable storage was established for /srv/data, a snapshot was taken, the directory was materially changed, and its exact earlier content and metadata were restored. The independent restart protocol was incomplete and does not change task functionality because restart persistence was not part of the public contract, but it materially limits confidence in the final operational state.","limitations":["The restart protocol lacks a different post-restart boot identity and recovered service, mount, data, and system-health observations. The fixed baseline boot identity was d052eb57-c81d-4309-ae7f-bce362073ff9, while the after-executor boot-identity probe received a connection reset.","Because node1 remained unreachable after reboot, persistence of the new loop-backed LVM activation, /srv/data mount, and restored data across restart is indeterminate.","After-executor lifecycle probes could not distinguish a node boot failure from a management-channel failure, and could not inspect failed services, listening sockets, temporary residue, or package health."]}
